{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>Thus</b> — a parable to that which is written in the Torah: \"If it be torn in pieces, let him bring it for evidence\" (Exodus 22:12)." "<b>corner of a bed</b> — it has no companion. And the sages of Spain said: one side (אזן אחת), and so none escapes except he who is sick and did not go to battle, and this is \"and who sits on the edge of a bed.\"" "<b>And the reason of \"on the edge\"</b> — for it is the custom of a person to place the bed in the house on one side, so they may put up a screen so it not be seen, and the sick person must lie in a place where there is no great light, for he cannot bear it. And know that the dalet is not among the letters that serve (לשרת), therefore it is never added either at the beginning of a word or at its end." "<b>And we know that \"in Damascus a couch\"</b> — is like \"on the edge of a bed,\" for ערש (couch) is like מטה (bed), and so the meaning is doubled; therefore דמשק is like בפאת (on the edge). Or the word is a compound of two, like לפלמוני, which otherwise would not exist, and so its meaning is: ובדמשק (in Damascus) is like \"and the son of my house who משק (draws water)\" — that is, he watches and does not move."], :book :amos, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {2 "The phrase אותיות שהן לשרת refers to the class of Hebrew letters that can function as root letters; Ibn Ezra's point is that dalet is always a root letter, never a superfluous addition.", 3 "The references to לפלמוני and to ובן משק ביתי (Genesis 15:2, where Ibn Ezra reads משק as related to שקד, to watch) are standard examples in his grammatical writings of words he analyzes as compounds or quasi-compounds."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Amos -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/amos"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:18:02.000633176Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}